# Home-page one-liners - the checks to run before `home.html` reaches Sathya

One screen. The rules, the before/after edits they came from and the version history are in `references/rules.md` in this folder - read that once at the start, then run this list on every entry's one-liner. Every line traces to a section there, named in parentheses. Read `writing-foundation` first. When a rule changes in `references/rules.md`, its test changes here in the same edit (and the other way round).

## On every entry's one-liner

- It names one concrete thing the student has made by the end of the item, something they can look at and check they have, not an outcome they are said to have gained (`an understanding of`, `experience of`, `having demonstrated`) and not a count of form boxes. (One thing the student has made)
- The thing includes the part that shows the student's thinking: the reasons, the check they ran, the difference they named, the habit they chose. (The learning is in the thing)
- It is a full plain sentence a student reads once, not a clipped noun phrase that assumes the reader will unpack it (`the path you chose, and why`; `the quantity you will predict, and from what`). (Full plain sentence)
- Every course concept is written in full, self-contained terms (`a physical system and its quantitative relationship`, `the conditions it holds under`, `a physics learning goal`), with no form shorthand (`from what`, `the condition it needs`, `written as a prediction`) that requires opening the form to decode. (Full course terms)
- Where the entry has two halves — *how you learned* and *what you learned* in a sprint story, or *closed-book written exam* and *partner conversation* in an exam — both halves are named. (Both halves)
- It adds what the title leaves unsaid instead of repeating the title's words, and carries no filler tags (`actually`, `in your words`, `in your own words`, `your own read of`). (No title echo or filler)
- The thing named is the thing the next item in the chain starts from, but the line names the thing itself, not the next item's use of it (`one scenario you can walk through on the exam` fails; `three real situations where your relationship predicts correctly, and two that look alike but where it does not` passes). (The chain, without naming the next step)
